# Code Quality

- Do not add unnecessary comments. Comments are only allowed when they explain non-obvious business logic, security reasoning, architecture decisions, or operational constraints.
- Keep formatting and style consistent with the existing codebase.
- Do not introduce unused helper functions, components, variables, CSS classes, or infrastructure resources.
- Prefer clear, readable implementation over clever code.
- Include proper error handling.
- Include structured, useful error logging.
- Do not log secrets, tokens, credentials, PII, or sensitive request/response payloads.
